  {
   "@id": "http://localhost:8080/how-much-do-movers-cost-in-philadelphia/#faq",
   "@type": "FAQPage",
   "mainEntity": [
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "On the calculator's planning rates, a local crew with a truck costs $100–$150 an hour for 2 movers and $250–$375 an hour for 5, which is $50–$75 for each mover. A 3-bedroom rowhouse usually takes 4. Those hourly figures are the core of how much do movers cost in Philadelphia, and stairs or a long carry add hours to them."
     },
     "name": "How much do movers cost in Philadelphia per hour?"
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Tipping is your choice. Published guides suggest $20.00–$65.00 for each mover for a full working day, less for a half day, or a share of the total bill. Tip at the high end after a day of narrow rowhouse stairs, and let the tip calculator on this page total it for your crew."
     },
     "name": "How much should each mover get as a tip?"
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The usual extras are stairs, a long carry from a truck that couldn't park close, minimum hours, travel time from the depot, packing materials and any parking permit. Ask each mover to name every fee on the written estimate, so how much do movers cost in Philadelphia on the day matches the quote."
     },
     "name": "What hidden costs can show up on a Philadelphia moving bill?"
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A home of about 1,000 square feet usually has 2 bedrooms, so start from $720–$1,800 for a local move of 10 road miles, off peak with no stairs or extras. A rowhouse that size often has two narrow flights, which pushes it up the range. Enter your bedrooms and stairs in the calculator for your own figure."
     },
     "name": "What does a 1,000-square-foot rowhouse cost to move?"
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "For a move within Pennsylvania, check that the company holds authority from the Pennsylvania Public Utility Commission. For a move to New Jersey or farther, look up its USDOT number with FMCSA. Be wary of an estimate given sight unseen, a demand for a large cash deposit, or blank papers to sign."
     },
     "name": "How can you tell if a Philadelphia mover is legitimate?"
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}